---
// The ONE place the site opens a video in a popover.
//
// Wistia's own class-based popover, which is what it is built for: the runtime
// reads its configuration off the CLASS NAME of the wrapper below, binds the
// <a> inside it (`popoverContent=link`) and opens the film in its lightbox.
// Those classes are PARSED BY WISTIA, not matched by CSS — nothing anywhere may
// target `.wistia_embed` or `wistia_async_*`.
//
// DRIVING IT FROM THE JS API INSTEAD DOES NOT WORK, and the reason is worth
// keeping. `Wistia.api(id).popover.show()` needs an embed that was initialised
// WITH popover options and IS IN LAYOUT. A `display: none` host initialises
// without them and then throws `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading
// 'popoverborderradius')` when shown; flipping its display afterwards does not
// re-initialise it. Measured, not assumed.
//
// THE TRIGGER IS THE CALLER'S <a> WITH A REAL href. Wistia binds the click; if
// its runtime is blocked or JS is off, the href still reaches the film. An
// inert control is the shape `TeamCard.astro` and `ProseLink.astro` argue
// against.
//
// WISTIA REWRITES WHAT IS INSIDE THIS WRAPPER — it moves the <a> into a
// `div.wistia_click_to_play` of its own. Two consequences the callers have to
// live with, both fixed rather than worked around:
//   1. That div becomes the grid/flex item, not the caller's <a>. Any root that
//      relied on a tag default for `display` collapses to 0x0 — which is what
//      emptied `.faq__video` and `.vcard`. Both declare `display: block` now.
//   2. Re-creating the nodes drops any per-node load listener. That is fixed in
//      Layout, where `.lazy-fade` lives: it delegates from the document with
//      capture now, so replacing a node cannot detach it. Timed re-runs used to
//      paper over it here and could not — a lazy image decodes when the reader
//      scrolls to it, which is long after any timer.
//
// YOUTUBE GETS NO POPOVER, deliberately. Wistia's runtime only plays Wistia
// media; adding YouTube's iframe API would mean a second video runtime plus the
// cookie conversation `videoEmbedUrl` documents. A `youtube` record renders its
// link with nothing around it.
import {
  WISTIA_RUNTIME,
  wistiaMediaScript,
  wistiaPopoverClass,
  type VideoRef,
} from "../../lib/video";

interface Props {
  video: VideoRef;
}

const { video } = Astro.props;
const isWistia = video.provider === "wistia";
---

{
  isWistia ? (
    <span class:list={["vpop", wistiaPopoverClass(video.id)]}>
      <slot />
    </span>
  ) : (
    <slot />
  )
}

{
  /* `is:inline`, so Astro leaves a third-party runtime it cannot see alone.
     Both are `async`; neither blocks paint. Loaded per embed rather than from
     Layout, so the ~300 pages with no video never fetch a video player.

     KNOWN COST: a page with N popovers emits N identical pairs. Markup waste
     rather than a bug — same URL, so the browser fetches each once, and
     E-v1.js is idempotent. TODO(video): dedupe via a `hasVideo` prop on Layout
     if it matters. NOT a module-level flag here: Astro renders every page in
     one process, so that state leaks and every page after the first loses its
     player. */
  isWistia && (
    <>
      <script is:inline async src={wistiaMediaScript(video.id)} />
      <script is:inline async src={WISTIA_RUNTIME} />
    </>
  )
}


<style>
  /* Carries Wistia's config classes and nothing else, so it must not affect
     layout: `display: contents` takes it out of the box tree and whatever
     Wistia puts inside inherits the caller's place in its parent's grid or
     flex row. */
  .vpop {
    display: contents;
  }
</style>
